# Quine

A **quine** is a program that takes no input and writes its own source code to
its output. It is the standard example of self-reproduction: a representation
that reproduces itself. In this vault it marks a precise boundary. A quine
reproduces a description of itself and says nothing about that description,
so it is self-referential without being a [[strange-loop]].

## Description

### The construction

A quine cannot simply contain its own text, since the text would then have to
contain itself. It works by holding a piece of data that describes the rest of
the program and using that data twice: once as instructions for what to print,
and once as the thing printed. The same double use, of a code as both a name
and the thing named, is what [[godel-numbering]] gives arithmetic. The name
"quine" was given by Hofstadter in *Gödel, Escher, Bach*, after the logician
W. V. O. Quine.

That quines exist in every sufficiently expressive language is a theorem, the
[[kleene-recursion-theorem]]. A quine is a [[fixed-point]]: a program $p$ whose
execution returns $p$.

### How far it can be pushed

The question asked in the discussion was how far programs that write
themselves can be pushed, in particular programs that translate themselves
into another language. A program in language A may print the source of a
program in language B, which in turn prints the original program in A:

$$p_A\xrightarrow{\mathrm{Exec}_A}p_B\xrightarrow{\mathrm{Exec}_B}p_A.$$

This is a [[cross-language-quine]]. The cycle can be made as long as one
likes, through as many languages as one likes, and it remains the same kind of
object: a closed chain of representations, each producing the next.

The observation attached to the question was that language models make the
trick unnecessary. A model translates a program from one language to another
on request, so the artful part of a cross-language quine, encoding one
language inside another by hand, is no longer needed to move a program between
representations. What remains interesting is not the translation but the
closure: that the chain returns to its start.

### What a quine does not do

The discussion drew the line in one sentence: a quine reproduces a
description, a reflective interpreter can reason about a description, and
neither implies incompleteness.

- **Against reflective computation.** A [[metacircular-interpreter]] takes a
  program as data and can inspect, run or transform it. A quine only copies.
  It has access to its own text and does nothing with it except emit it.
- **Against the Gödel sentence.** The [[godel-sentence]] uses the same
  self-application as a quine, and then asserts something about the result,
  namely that it is not provable. The assertion is what produces a theorem. A
  quine asserts nothing.
- **Against a strange loop.** A quine has a cycle, source to output to source,
  and no change of level. The output is the same kind of thing as the input.

In the [[taxonomy-of-self-reference]] the quine is the type specimen of class
B, self-reproduction, between plain recursion and formal diagonalization.

## Relations

- It is a kind of [[self-reference]] {relation: is-a}, by reproduction and not by assertion.
- It is a [[fixed-point]] {relation: example-of} of program execution.
- It belongs to class B of the [[taxonomy-of-self-reference]] {relation: part-of}.
- It contrasts with the [[metacircular-interpreter]] {relation: contrasts}: copying a description against reasoning about one.
- It uses the same double use of code as [[godel-numbering]] {relation: analogous-to}.
- It stops short of the [[godel-sentence]] {relation: distinguished-from}, which adds a claim about itself.
- It is not a [[strange-loop]] {relation: distinguished-from}: it closes a cycle on one level.
- It does not lead to the [[incompleteness-theorems]] {relation: distinguished-from}; self-reproduction alone has no such consequence.
- It bears on the question [[building-a-loop-in-hmd]] {relation: answers}: a card that regenerates itself would be a quine and nothing more.

## Neighbouring concepts

- **[[metacircular-interpreter]].** An interpreter for a language written in
  that language. It treats programs as data to be examined, where a quine
  treats its own text as data to be copied.
- **[[godel-sentence]].** A sentence of arithmetic equivalent to the statement
  of its own unprovability. It is a quine that has been given something to say.
- **[[fixed-point]].** A value that a transformation maps to itself. Quines
  are the fixed points of "run this program and take its output".
- **[[taxonomy-of-self-reference]].** Eight classes of self-reference by
  mechanism. The quine is the example for self-reproduction.

